{
  "$schema": "../../schema/topic.schema.json",
  "id": "iterators",
  "title": "Iterators & closures",
  "primer": {
    "title": "Lazy pipelines, pulled from the end",
    "body": [
      "An iterator does nothing until something asks it for items. Adaptors like `map` and `filter` only *describe* work; consumers like `sum`, `count`, `collect` and `for` pull values through.",
      "`next()` returns `Some(item)` and advances, or `None` once exhausted. An iterator you have partly consumed continues where it stopped.",
      "`collect` builds whatever the annotation asks for. From items of type `Result<T, E>` it can build a `Vec<Result<T, E>>` (keep everything) or a `Result<Vec<T>, E>` (stop at the first `Err`).",
      "Ranges are iterators: `2..=n` includes `n`, `2..n` does not. `product()` of an empty range is 1, `sum()` is 0."
    ]
  },
  "exercises": [
    {
      "id": "next-advances",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "predict-output",
      "timeBudget": 60,
      "source": "rustlings: iterators1",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": [
        "fn main() {",
        "    let fruits = [\"banana\", \"custard apple\", \"avocado\"];",
        "",
        "    let mut it = fruits.iter();",
        "    it.next();",
        "    let second = it.next();",
        "    let rest: Vec<_> = it.collect();",
        "",
        "    println!(\"{second:?} {rest:?}\");",
        "    println!(\"{:?}\", fruits.iter().nth(3));",
        "}"
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "Some(\"custard apple\") [\"avocado\"]\nNone"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "code": [
            "Some(\"custard apple\") [\"avocado\"]",
            "None"
          ],
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "Each `next()` advances `it`, so `collect` only sees what is left. `fruits.iter()` is a fresh iterator; index 3 is past the end, so `nth(3)` is `None`."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "code": [
            "Some(\"banana\") [\"custard apple\", \"avocado\"]",
            "None"
          ],
          "rationale": "The `next()` on line 5 consumed `\"banana\"`, even though its result was thrown away."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "code": [
            "Some(\"custard apple\") [\"banana\", \"custard apple\", \"avocado\"]",
            "None"
          ],
          "rationale": "`collect` does not rewind. It drains the iterator from its current position."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "code": [
            "Some(\"custard apple\") [\"avocado\"]",
            "Some(\"avocado\")"
          ],
          "rationale": "`nth` counts from 0, so `nth(3)` asks for a fourth element."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "capitalize-first",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "predict-output",
      "timeBudget": 90,
      "source": "rustlings: iterators2",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": [
        "fn capitalize_first(input: &str) -> String {",
        "    let mut chars = input.chars();",
        "    match chars.next() {",
        "        None => String::new(),",
        "        Some(first) => {",
        "            let up: String = first.to_uppercase().collect();",
        "            up + chars.as_str()",
        "        }",
        "    }",
        "}",
        "",
        "fn main() {",
        "    let words = [\"hello\", \" \", \"\", \"world\"];",
        "    let out: Vec<String> =",
        "        words.iter().map(|w| capitalize_first(w)).collect();",
        "",
        "    println!(\"{out:?}\");",
        "}"
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "[\"Hello\", \" \", \"\", \"World\"]"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "`[\"Hello\", \" \", \"\", \"World\"]`",
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "`chars.next()` takes the first character; `as_str()` is whatever remains. For `\"\"` the match hits `None` and returns an empty `String` — `map` still yields one item per input."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "`[\"Hello\", \" \", \"World\"]`",
          "rationale": "`map` is one-to-one. An empty result string is still an element."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "`[\"HELLO\", \" \", \"\", \"WORLD\"]`",
          "rationale": "Only `first` is upper-cased. The rest of the word is appended unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "It panics on the empty string",
          "rationale": "Nothing unwraps here: the `None` arm handles a string with no first character."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "collect-results",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "predict-output",
      "timeBudget": 90,
      "source": "rustlings: iterators3",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": [
        "fn divide(a: i64, b: i64) -> Result<i64, String> {",
        "    if b == 0 {",
        "        return Err(String::from(\"divide by zero\"));",
        "    }",
        "    Ok(a / b)",
        "}",
        "",
        "fn main() {",
        "    let divisors = [2, 0, 4];",
        "",
        "    let each: Vec<Result<i64, String>> =",
        "        divisors.iter().map(|d| divide(8, *d)).collect();",
        "    let all: Result<Vec<i64>, String> =",
        "        divisors.iter().map(|d| divide(8, *d)).collect();",
        "",
        "    println!(\"{}\", each.len());",
        "    println!(\"{all:?}\");",
        "}"
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "3\nErr(\"divide by zero\")"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "code": [
            "3",
            "Err(\"divide by zero\")"
          ],
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "Same pipeline, two target types. A `Vec<Result>` keeps all three outcomes. A `Result<Vec>` is `Ok` only if every item is `Ok`; otherwise it is the first `Err`."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "code": [
            "3",
            "Ok([4, 2])"
          ],
          "rationale": "Collecting into `Result<Vec<_>, _>` does not skip failures — one `Err` makes the whole result an `Err`."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "code": [
            "2",
            "Err(\"divide by zero\")"
          ],
          "rationale": "`each` has one entry per divisor, including the `Err`."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "It does not compile: `collect` cannot produce two different types from the same pipeline",
          "rationale": "`collect` is generic over its result. The annotations on lines 11 and 13 choose the implementation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "factorial-as-product",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "pick-refactor",
      "timeBudget": 75,
      "source": "rustlings: iterators4",
      "prompt": "Which replacement for the highlighted lines keeps the behaviour identical?",
      "code": [
        "fn factorial(num: u64) -> u64 {",
        "    let mut result = 1;",
        "    for i in 2..=num {",
        "        result *= i;",
        "    }",
        "    result",
        "}",
        "",
        "fn main() {",
        "    println!(\"{} {}\", factorial(0), factorial(5));",
        "}"
      ],
      "focus": [
        2,
        6
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "1 120"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "code": [
            "(2..=num).product()"
          ],
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "`product` multiplies every item, and the product of an empty range is 1 — exactly what the loop gives for 0 and 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "code": [
            "(1..num).product()"
          ],
          "rationale": "`1..num` stops before `num`: `factorial(5)` becomes 24."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "code": [
            "(2..=num).sum()"
          ],
          "rationale": "That adds instead of multiplying, and the empty sum is 0, not 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "code": [
            "(0..=num).product()"
          ],
          "rationale": "Starting at 0 makes every product 0."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "flat-map-count",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "predict-output",
      "timeBudget": 90,
      "source": "rustlings: iterators5",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": [
        "use std::collections::HashMap;",
        "",
        "#[derive(PartialEq)]",
        "enum Progress {",
        "    Started,",
        "    Complete,",
        "}",
        "",
        "fn main() {",
        "    use Progress::*;",
        "    let day1 =",
        "        HashMap::from([(\"var\", Complete), (\"fn\", Started)]);",
        "    let day2 =",
        "        HashMap::from([(\"if\", Complete), (\"as\", Complete)]);",
        "    let days = [day1, day2];",
        "",
        "    let done = days",
        "        .iter()",
        "        .flat_map(|day| day.values())",
        "        .filter(|p| **p == Complete)",
        "        .count();",
        "    let all: usize = days.iter().map(|d| d.len()).sum();",
        "",
        "    println!(\"{done} of {all}\");",
        "}"
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "3 of 4"
      },
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "`3 of 4`",
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "`flat_map` strings the values of both maps into one sequence of four; three of them are `Complete`."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "`2 of 2`",
          "rationale": "That counts maps. `flat_map` yields the *values inside* each map, not the maps."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "`3 of 2`",
          "rationale": "`all` sums each map's `len()`: 2 + 2."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "It does not compile: `**p` dereferences too many times",
          "rationale": "`values()` yields `&Progress`, and `filter` passes a reference to that: `&&Progress`. Two `*` reach the value."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "filter-sum-shadow",
      "type": "choice",
      "flavor": "predict-output",
      "timeBudget": 45,
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": [
        "fn main() {",
        "    let words = vec![\"apple\", \"fig\", \"banana\", \"kiwi\"];",
        "",
        "    let total: usize = words",
        "        .iter()",
        "        .filter(|w| w.len() > 3)",
        "        .map(|w| w.len())",
        "        .sum();",
        "",
        "    let total = total * 2;",
        "    let label = if total > 30 { \"big\" } else { \"small\" };",
        "",
        "    println!(\"{total} {label}\");",
        "}"
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "`30 small`",
          "correct": true,
          "rationale": "apple (5) + banana (6) + kiwi (4) = 15, shadowed to 30. The test is strictly `> 30`, so the label is `small`."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "`30 big`",
          "rationale": "The sum is right, but `30 > 30` is false."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "`36 big`",
          "rationale": "This counts `fig` too. Its length is 3 and the filter keeps only lengths greater than 3."
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "It does not compile: `total` is declared twice",
          "rationale": "Re-declaring with `let` is shadowing, which is legal."
        }
      ],
      "verify": {
        "stdout": "30 small"
      }
    }
  ]
}
